## Naming Convention — Consistent Everywhere

```typescript
// Variables & functions: camelCase
const activeUsers = users.filter(u => u.isActive);
function calculateTotalPrice(cartItems: CartItem[]): number {}

// Types & interfaces: PascalCase
interface UserProfile {}
type ApiResponse<T> = {}

// Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
const MAX_RETRY_COUNT = 3;

// Files: kebab-case
// user-profile.service.ts | use-auth.hook.ts | api-client.ts

// Boolean variables: prefix with is/has/can/should
const isLoading = false;
const hasPermission = true;
```

**Rule: Naming must be self-documenting. No abbreviations except universally known ones (id, url, api).**

---

## Single Responsibility (SRP)

Each function / class / module does ONE thing only.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function processUser(user: User) {
  validateUser(user);
  saveToDatabase(user);
  sendEmail(user);
  logActivity(user);
}

// ✅ GOOD — orchestrator calls focused units
async function onboardUser(user: User) {
  validate(user);
  await userRepository.save(user);
  await emailService.sendWelcome(user);
  logger.info('user_onboarded', { userId: user.id });
}
```

---

## DRY — No Repeated Logic

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
if (user.role === 'admin' || user.role === 'moderator') { /* check 1 */ }
if (user.role === 'admin' || user.role === 'moderator') { /* check 2 */ }

// ✅ GOOD
const MODERATOR_ROLES = ['admin', 'moderator'] as const;
const canModerate = (user: User) => MODERATOR_ROLES.includes(user.role);
```

---

## KISS — Simplicity Wins

Prefer readable over clever.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — clever but unreadable
const result = arr.reduce((a, b) => ({...a, [b.id]: (a[b.id] || 0) + b.val}), {});

// ✅ GOOD — obvious at a glance
const result: Record<string, number> = {};
for (const item of arr) {
  result[item.id] = (result[item.id] ?? 0) + item.val;
}
```

---

## Fail Fast — Validate at Entry

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — guard clauses first
function processOrder(order: Order) {
  if (!order) throw new Error('Order is required');
  if (!order.items?.length) throw new Error('Order must have items');
  if (order.total < 0) throw new Error('Order total cannot be negative');

  const discounted = applyDiscount(order.total);
  return createInvoice(order, discounted);
}
```

---

## Pure Functions — No Hidden Side Effects

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — mutates input silently
function getFullName(user: User) {
  user.lastAccessed = new Date(); // hidden side effect
  return `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`;
}

// ✅ GOOD
function getFullName(user: Pick<User, 'firstName' | 'lastName'>): string {
  return `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`;
}
```

---

## Error Handling

```typescript
// Always handle errors explicitly — never swallow them silently
try {
  const data = await fetchUser(id);
  return data;
} catch (error) {
  logger.error('fetch_user_failed', { userId: id, error });
  throw new AppError('USER_FETCH_FAILED', { cause: error });
}

// Use typed errors, not generic strings
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(public code: string, options?: ErrorOptions) {
    super(code, options);
  }
}
```

---

## File Structure — Always in This Order

```
1. Imports (external → internal → relative)
2. Constants / Types / Interfaces
3. Private helpers
4. Main exported functions / components
5. Default export (if applicable)
```

---

## Comments — English Only

```typescript
// ✅ Explain WHY, not WHAT
// Retry up to 3 times because the payment gateway has intermittent timeouts
const MAX_PAYMENT_RETRIES = 3;

// ❌ Don't explain obvious code
// increment i by 1
i++;
```

