## Frontend Architecture — UI vs Business Logic

Prefer a **container → hook → UI** separation for non-trivial screens.

```
src/
  hooks/
    use-canvas-editor.ts        ← business/editor logic, refs, actions
  components/
    layout/
      MainContent.tsx           ← container/orchestrator, layout + wiring only
      Toolbar.tsx               ← UI only, no Fabric/DOM mutations
  utils/
    fabric-selection.ts         ← pure helpers to read Fabric selection
  types/
    text-toolbar.types.ts       ← shared UI state/types (no React imports)
```

- **UI components** (`Toolbar`, buttons, panels):
  - Receive data + callbacks via props
  - Do **not** reach into canvas refs or call imperative APIs directly
- **Hooks** (`useXxx`):
  - Own state + refs
  - Expose `props` + `actions` objects for easy wiring
  - Keep functions small; extract pure helpers to `utils/` when logic grows
- **Containers** (`MainContent`):
  - Keep JSX clean (avoid long inline callbacks)
  - Compose hooks + pass bindings down

Rule of thumb:
- If a component needs to call `canvas.getActiveObject()` / `target.set()` / `requestRenderAll()`, that code belongs in a **hook/service**, not in the UI component.

